(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man has been intubated and mechanically ventilated for 3 days after a motor vehicle crash. He develops a new fever, purulent endotracheal secretions and a new infiltrate. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Health care-associated pneumonia","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Community-acquired pneumonia","Hospital-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ventilator-associated pneumonia develops more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation, and he has been ventilated for 3 days. Colonized secretions inhaled through the tube cause it, and it carries higher mortality and a longer intensive care stay. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 48"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has 12 mm of induration on a tuberculin skin test. His chest radiograph shows a fibrotic upper-lobe lesion, he does not recall ever being treated for tuberculosis, and he has no symptoms. Which step must come before latent-infection treatment?","choices":["Exclusion of active tuberculosis disease","A repeat skin test in 8 to 10 weeks","A second chest radiograph in 1 year","An interferon-gamma release assay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrotic lesions warrant review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease, and tuberculosis disease must be excluded before latent-infection treatment begins. Imaging cannot reliably determine activity, so microbiologic testing answers that question. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29, 37"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with asthma wakes with cough and mild wheeze. Her peak flow is 70% of her personal best, placing her in the yellow zone of her written action plan. What does the plan direct her to do first?","choices":["Go to the hospital now","Stop her controller until she feels better","Add quick-relief medicine and keep her controller","Start an oral steroid right away"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the yellow zone, 50% to 79% of personal best, asthma is getting worse: she adds quick-relief medicine (a short-acting beta-2 agonist) and keeps taking her green-zone controller. An oral steroid is added only if she does not return to green after an hour. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who drank methanol has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does his carbon dioxide show?","choices":["An added respiratory alkalosis","Impending respiratory failure","An added respiratory acidosis","Appropriate compensation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2, so 27 to 31. His 29 sits inside that range, so the fall in carbon dioxide is the expected respiratory compensation for his elevated-gap metabolic acidosis from methanol, with no second disorder. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 55, 58, 59"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with asthma had facial flushing, red eyes and wheezing several hours after taking aspirin last month. He now has low back pain from lifting. Which medication should he avoid?","choices":["Meloxicam","Albuterol","Ibuprofen","Celecoxib"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flushing, conjunctival irritation and wheeze after aspirin, even when delayed up to 12 hours, mark aspirin-sensitive asthma. Ibuprofen is a non-selective anti-inflammatory drug that cross-reacts and must be avoided; selective agents may be used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with known hypercalcemia has a metabolic alkalosis, a high urine chloride and a normal blood pressure. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 45 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). In which group of metabolic alkalosis does she belong?","choices":["Post-hypercapnic","Saline responsive","Saline unresponsive, hypertensive","Saline unresponsive, normotensive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high urine chloride (above 40 mEq/L) marks saline-unresponsive alkalosis; with a normal blood pressure it is the normotensive group, which lists hypoparathyroidism and hypercalcemia and severe potassium depletion. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 41, 42, 43"},{"q":"Which psychiatric conditions appear in the differential diagnosis of asthma?","choices":["Conversion and emotional laryngeal wheeze","Depression and insomnia","Generalized anxiety and panic disorder","Bipolar disorder and schizophrenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Psychiatric causes in the differential are conversion disorders, emotional laryngeal wheezing and, rarely, malingering or factitious disorder. They can mimic asthma, which is one reason an objective lung function test is part of the diagnosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with type 2 diabetes treated with metformin received several liters of normal saline during abdominal surgery. He has had no diarrhea. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 33 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 141 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which explains this blood gas?","choices":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","The large saline infusion","His metformin","Postoperative hypoventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a low bicarbonate is metabolic acidosis; the gap is 141 - (114 + 17) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Saline infusion is a listed normal-gap cause (chloride retention). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is reviewed for comorbidities that become more important with age. Which condition should be added to his screening at this age?","choices":["Nasal polyps","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Osteoporosis and falls","Allergic rhinitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Comorbidities in older adults add osteoporosis, frailty, falls and cognitive decline to cardiovascular disease, metabolic disease and depression. Inhaled corticosteroid-related fracture risk adds to the concern about bone health. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 53"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a positive interferon-gamma release assay. She also reports 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss. Which action should be avoided at this point?","choices":["Ordering a chest radiograph","Collecting three sputum specimens","Starting latent-infection treatment now","Asking about her exposure history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis disease must be excluded before latent-infection treatment, and a positive result requires evaluation for disease before preventive treatment. Her cough, night sweats and weight loss make disease possible, so she needs chest imaging and sputum testing first. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 37"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 17 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2. She has had no exacerbations in the past year. Which initial maintenance regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroid plus beta agonist","Long-acting beta agonist plus antimuscarinic","Long-term azithromycin","A single short-acting bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A score of 17 and a dyspnea grade of 2 mean more symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk, which is group B. Group B starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic, ideally in a single inhaler for convenience. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has just finished a 5-day course of oral corticosteroids for an asthma exacerbation and feels well. He asks whether he must slowly taper the medicine. Which reply is correct?","choices":["He should continue it long term","He must taper over the next 2 weeks","He should switch to an inhaled form for 5 days","No taper is needed after a short course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroids are used for control during exacerbations as a short course, and no taper is required. Long-term systemic therapy is different; it is reserved for severe or refractory cases at the minimum dose needed. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"In acute bronchitis, what does green or yellow sputum indicate?","choices":["No clue to a viral or bacterial cause","A bacterial cause needing antibiotics","Pertussis infection","Progression to pneumonia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Colored mucus can occur in acute bronchitis but does not differentiate viral from bacterial infection. Purulent or colored sputum does not establish bacterial infection and does not justify antibiotics. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 18"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman develops an acute respiratory acidosis after aspiration pneumonitis two hours ago. When will her kidneys begin to compensate?","choices":["After weeks, completing over months","In 6 to 12 hours, completing over days","They do not; the lungs compensate instead","Within minutes, completing in an hour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate; this begins 6 to 12 hours after onset, and complete compensation may take days. At two hours her bicarbonate is near normal. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Spirometry shows obstruction and the forced vital capacity is also low, raising the possibility of a mixed disorder. Which measurement confirms it?","choices":["Diffusing capacity","Arterial blood gas","Total lung capacity","A repeat bronchodilator response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When an obstructed study also has a low forced vital capacity, a mixed obstructive and restrictive disorder is possible and is confirmed with total lung capacity. Lung volumes identify hyperinflation or coexisting restriction. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 17 mg/dL, respiratory rate 34/min and blood pressure 132/56 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 + urea 0 (17 is not above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (34 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (a diastolic of 56 is 60 or lower, even with a normal systolic) + age 1 (81) = 3. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital and urgent senior review. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which cause of acute respiratory acidosis is a central nervous system depressant?","choices":["Intravenous sedation","Caffeine excess","Thyroxine excess","Salicylate toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central nervous system depression from recreational drugs, an intracranial catastrophe or intravenous sedation causes acute respiratory acidosis by suppressing breathing, so carbon dioxide is retained. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman who is 14 weeks pregnant comes in during October and asks about vaccines. Which vaccine should be avoided during her pregnancy?","choices":["Recombinant influenza vaccine","Inactivated influenza vaccine","Tdap during this pregnancy","Live attenuated influenza vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In pregnancy, influenza vaccination uses an age-appropriate inactivated or recombinant vaccine in any trimester, and the live attenuated vaccine is avoided. Live attenuated vaccine has pregnancy and immunocompromise restrictions, so her pregnancy rules it out. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 124"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who is 24 weeks pregnant has had 3 days of fever, myalgias and cough, and she is not short of breath. Influenza is strongly suspected, and a molecular test has been sent. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Start oseltamivir now","A single dose of baloxavir","Await molecular confirmation","No antiviral after 48 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pregnancy in any trimester is a high-risk group, and oseltamivir is preferred for high-risk patients, including in pregnancy. It is started promptly, even after 48 hours of symptoms, without waiting for confirmation in a high-risk patient. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 98, 108, 95, 105"},{"q":"A 66-year-old thin man with a long smoking history breathes through pursed lips. He has a barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance to percussion. His diffusing capacity is reduced, and he produces little sputum. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Chronic bronchitis subtype","Emphysema subtype","Pulmonary fibrosis","Asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A thin patient with pursed-lip breathing, barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance, little sputum, and a reduced diffusing capacity is the classic emphysema picture (the pink puffer), from alveolar destruction and loss of elastic recoil. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 15, 20"},{"q":"Which compensates more for the same change in carbon dioxide: acute or chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Neither; the lungs compensate","Chronic","Acute","They compensate equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate rises about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide in acute respiratory acidosis, but about 3.5 mEq/L per 10 in chronic. Renal compensation takes days to complete, so chronic compensates more. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"After a positive tuberculin skin test, what separates latent infection from tuberculosis disease?","choices":["Clinical evaluation and imaging","A follow-up blood assay","The size of the induration","A repeat skin test in 8 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neither the skin test nor the interferon-gamma release assay distinguishes latent infection from disease, so a positive result requires clinical evaluation and chest imaging; symptoms or suspicious imaging then require microbiologic testing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 22, 23"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has asthma symptoms throughout every day, uses his rescue inhaler several times a day, wakes with symptoms nearly every night, and is extremely limited in daily activities. His forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 52% predicted. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms throughout the day, rescue use several times a day, night awakenings often 7 times a week, extreme limitation and a 1-second volume below 60% predicted define severe persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with minimal smoking history has emphysema with a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.61. His chest x-ray shows the changes mainly in the lower lobes. Which test is most appropriate to find the cause?","choices":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin level and genotype","Computed tomography of the chest","Arterial blood gas","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients under 45, or without significant exposure, should be evaluated for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and basilar emphysema adds to the suspicion. A level below 11 micromol/L with a severely deficient genotype is diagnostic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 30"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted to the intensive care unit with severe community-acquired pneumonia. Pseudomonas grew from his sputum during an admission last year. Which agent provides reliable antipseudomonal coverage?","choices":["Vancomycin","Moxifloxacin","Cefepime","Ertapenem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prior respiratory isolation of Pseudomonas is assessed as a resistant-pathogen risk, so an active antipseudomonal agent is added. An antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam is used; moxifloxacin and ertapenem are not reliable options for Pseudomonas. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with ventilator-associated pneumonia has had an adequate response to culture-directed antibiotics. What is the usual total duration of therapy?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, a 7-day course is usual when the response is adequate, adjusted to clinical response, with prompt de-escalation as cultures clarify the diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 49"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with septic shock has a lactate elevation. Sodium 132 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 96 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is her anion gap?","choices":["24","28","36","48"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = 132 - (96 + 12) = 132 - 108 = 24, above 9 to 15: elevated. Lactic acidosis, the L of the mnemonic, raises the gap through unmeasured anions. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation takes a long-acting beta agonist, a long-acting antimuscarinic and an inhaled corticosteroid; he rarely uses albuterol. His atrial fibrillation episodes and palpitations have become more frequent since his regimen was started. Which component is most likely contributing?","choices":["The inhaled corticosteroid","The long-acting beta agonist","The long-acting antimuscarinic","His occasional albuterol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta agonists produce sympathetic stimulation that can cause tachycardia and arrhythmias and can make atrial fibrillation worse; their cardiac effects are a listed concern in older adults. With rare albuterol use, the daily beta agonist is the likely driver. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 53"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman received a respiratory syncytial virus vaccine last year and asks whether she needs another dose this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["A second dose this fall","Revaccinate every 10 years","No repeat dose is recommended","Revaccinate every year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory syncytial virus vaccination is one dose for all adults aged 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 at increased risk, and repeat annual dosing is not currently recommended. She already had her one dose. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pet-shop employee has had a week of fever, severe headache, photophobia and a nonproductive cough. His pulse is 72/min with a temperature of 103°F, his spleen is palpable, and macular rashes on his face resemble the rose spots of typhoid fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mycoplasma infection","Influenza","Psittacosis","Acute histoplasmosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Psittacosis is an occupational infection of pet-shop and zoo employees and poultry workers, caught from birds such as parakeets. Severe headache, photophobia, a dry cough, relative bradycardia, splenomegaly and Horder spots (facial macular rashes like typhoid rose spots) complete the picture. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 83, 75, 76, 89, 99"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with Crohn disease has had profuse watery diarrhea for five days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.31 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 15 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 137 - (112 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride, so it is a normal-gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis. Bicarbonate loss in diarrhea fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 47, 52, 56"},{"q":"Which hazard of oxygen therapy is a risk for anyone near the patient, not only the patient?","choices":["Fire","Mucosal irritation","Hypercapnia","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fire is the hazard that threatens anyone near the patient, not only the patient, because oxygen is highly flammable. The others (hypercapnia, mucosal irritation, vasoconstriction) injure only the person receiving the oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who ingested ethylene glycol has an arterial pH of 6.62 (normal 7.35 to 7.45). Which complications does this pH threaten?","choices":["Seizures, cerebral edema, cardiac irritability","Reduced oxygen release from hemoglobin","Tetany from low ionized calcium","Hypokalemia from potassium entering cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable and may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures, cerebral edema and metabolic disturbances; it may warrant therapy to normalize the pH along with treating the cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman going home on oxygen asks what signs to post. Which signage is taught?","choices":["A sign with her pharmacy's number","No Smoking and Oxygen in Use signs","A sign with her oxygen flow rate","A sign with her recertification date"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to post No Smoking signage, and potentially Oxygen in Use signs, within the home. Oxygen is highly flammable, and the signs warn everyone who enters, including visitors and firefighters. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 93%, but during a walking test she drops to 85%. She is mobile and reports exertional breathlessness. Which oxygen plan is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen over 15 hours a day","Oxygen during exertion","No oxygen for now","Oxygen just at night"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe exertional desaturation, 88% or less with exertion, qualifies for long-term oxygen during exertion. Her resting saturation of 93% is moderate desaturation, without a resting indication, so oxygen is used with activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman completes the 8-item symptom assessment test, each item scored 0 to 5: cough 2, phlegm 1, chest tightness 1, breathlessness on a hill or flight of stairs 3, limitation of home activities 2, confidence leaving home 1, sleep 1, and energy 2. What is her total score?","choices":["11","12","13","14"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cough 2 + phlegm 1 + chest tightness 1 + breathlessness on stairs 3 + home activities 2 + confidence leaving home 1 + sleep 1 + energy 2 = 13. A total of 10 or more counts as more symptoms when assigning her group. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with alcoholism and chronic liver disease has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin in the past. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline as a single drug","Amoxicillin as a single drug","Levofloxacin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alcoholism and chronic liver disease are listed comorbidities, so he needs a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). His amoxicillin anaphylaxis removes the amoxicillin-based combination, leaving the fluoroquinolone, with a review of QT, interactions and fluoroquinolone risks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man describes cough and chest tightness with cold air and smoke over several months. His spirometry is normal, with no meaningful change after albuterol. Asthma is still suspected. Which test is most appropriate next?","choices":["Methacholine challenge","Exercise challenge","Repeat spirometry in 1 year","Chest x-ray"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation testing with methacholine or histamine is useful when spirometry is not diagnostic, as here, where a typical history meets normal spirometry. His normal lung function also clears the rule that provocation is not done below 65% predicted. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which three components establish the diagnosis of asthma?","choices":["History, chest radiograph, and allergy testing","History, physical exam, and objective lung function","Physical exam, serum immunoglobulin E, and sputum culture","Symptom response to an oral corticosteroid trial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is diagnosed from the history, the physical examination and an objective measurement of lung function. The objective measure matters because the exam can be normal between attacks, and it feeds both the diagnostic criteria and the severity classification. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with a panic attack is breathing fast, with perioral numbness and tingling hands. His roommate hands him a paper bag to breathe into. What is accurate education about the bag?","choices":["It safely corrects his carbon dioxide","It is unnecessary and may lower his oxygen","It is needed to prevent tetany","It works by raising his bicarbonate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Breathing into a paper bag is not necessary for hyperventilation: it fails to correct the carbon dioxide and may decrease oxygen, and any benefit is placebo. Hyperventilation is usually self-limited, and the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman with dysphagia after a stroke lives at home. She has 3 days of fever, a productive cough and a new right lower lobe infiltrate, and imaging shows no abscess or empyema. She is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere pneumonia. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated and has had no recent intravenous antibiotics. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Supportive care, no antibiotic","Cefepime plus vancomycin","Weeks of ampicillin-sulbactam","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, productive cough and an infiltrate in a patient at risk of aspiration is aspiration pneumonia, an infection treated with the regimen for its setting and severity: here nonsevere pneumonia from home on a ward, a beta-lactam plus a macrolide. With no abscess or empyema, anaerobic coverage is not added. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 50, 51, 56"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 8 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. She was admitted to the hospital once in the past year for an exacerbation. Using the 2020 four-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group C","Group B","Group D","Group A"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 8 and a dyspnea grade of 1 mean fewer symptoms, and 1 exacerbation leading to hospitalization means high risk. In the 2020 scheme, few symptoms with high risk is group C (in the current scheme she would be E). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a myocardial infarction and is placed on oxygen in the ambulance although his saturation was 98%. Which effect of oxygen makes this a problem?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Carbon dioxide retention","Fire risk","Mucosal irritation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His saturation was 98%, so he was not hypoxic. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. Giving it to a man with a normal saturation exposes him to that effect without benefit. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man on home oxygen at 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier complains of a sore, dry nose and occasional bleeding. Which change addresses the cause?","choices":["Apply a petroleum lip and nose balm","Add humidity to the oxygen","Switch to a non-rebreather mask","Increase the flow to 6 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is on 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier, and has a sore, dry nose with bleeding. That is mucosal irritation. Humidity is used with cannula flow rates greater than 4 L/min, so adding humidity to the oxygen treats the cause. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9, 15"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman shared an office with a coworker diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 2 weeks ago, and her exposure has now ended. She has no symptoms and no immune problem, and her interferon-gamma release assay today is negative. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["No further testing is needed","Repeat the test in 8 to 10 weeks","Begin latent-infection treatment","Three sputum specimens today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. She is symptom-free and immunocompetent, so no immediate disease evaluation is triggered. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 70% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 420 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2, and a body mass index of 25. Based on her BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index, what is her estimated 4-year survival?","choices":["18%","57%","67%","80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 70% (65 or more) = 0 + walk 420 meters (350 or more) = 0 + dyspnea grade 2 = 1 + body mass index 25 (over 21) = 0, for a total of 1. Scores of 0 to 2 predict about 80% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How is emphysema defined?","choices":["Narrowing of the bronchioles","Reversible bronchospasm","Chronic cough with sputum production","Destruction and enlargement of the alveoli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema is an anatomic, pathologic definition describing destruction and permanent enlargement of the alveoli, with air trapping. That separates it from chronic bronchitis, which is defined clinically by chronic cough and sputum. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"After a recent tuberculosis exposure, a first tuberculin skin test is negative. When is the test repeated?","choices":["2-4 weeks after the last exposure","4-6 weeks after the last exposure","8-10 weeks after the last exposure","16-20 weeks after the last exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or blood assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. Symptoms or high clinical suspicion still require disease evaluation now. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia confirmed on chest radiograph and is stable for outpatient care. Molecular testing is positive for a respiratory virus, and he can be seen again in 2 days. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Add azithromycin for atypicals","Amoxicillin despite the result","Admission for ceftriaxone","No antibiotic, close follow-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A viral-positive outpatient without comorbidities may avoid empiric antibiotics when close follow-up is reliable. His positive molecular test, absent comorbidities and arranged review in 2 days meet those conditions; antibiotics treat a bacterial syndrome, not a positive viral test. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 47, 126"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has new-onset persistent wheezing and cough that began last year. She has nasal polyps. Allergy skin tests are negative and her serum immunoglobulin E is normal. She had a wheezing episode after taking aspirin for a headache. Which type of asthma is most likely?","choices":["Catamenial asthma","Occupational asthma","Extrinsic asthma","Intrinsic asthma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adult onset, persistent symptoms, nasal polyps, negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E, and aspirin sensitivity all fit intrinsic asthma, the non-atopic form seen in about 10% of patients, which is usually more severe and persistent. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 35"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has had a chronic productive cough and weight loss. Her sputum acid-fast smear is positive. Which testing will identify the mycobacterial species?","choices":["A repeat acid-fast smear","Molecular testing and culture","Chest computed tomography","An interferon-gamma release assay"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive acid-fast smear supports mycobacteria but cannot distinguish tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria. The species is confirmed with molecular testing and culture, and it matters because transmission and treatment differ. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 24, 27, 39, 41"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has persistent asthma symptoms and frequent rescue inhaler use despite a prescribed inhaled corticosteroid plus long-acting beta-2 agonist. She has not been seen since the prescription 6 months ago. Which step should come first?","choices":["Refer for a monoclonal antibody","Check adherence and inhaler technique","Start long-term oral corticosteroids","Test for steroid-resistant asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and follow-up visits review medications, compliance and correct use before adjusting therapy. Her increased rescue use signals poor control, so adherence and technique are checked first. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 43, 90, 91"},{"q":"What is the main limitation of a positive Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction result?","choices":["It can detect colonization","It needs a lung biopsy specimen","It has low sensitivity","It cannot be run on induced sputum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymerase chain reaction for Pneumocystis on induced sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage is highly sensitive but can detect colonization, so a positive result is interpreted with the symptoms, the imaging and the organism burden rather than read as proof of pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 65, 67, 69"},{"q":"For at least how long is chronic cavitary histoplasmosis treated?","choices":["1 month","3 months","6 months","12 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic cavitary disease requires prolonged azole therapy, usually at least 12 months. The current updates replace a simple 4-week symptom rule for deciding treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with a body mass index of 58 has loud snoring, witnessed pauses in breathing at night and daytime sleepiness. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 55 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.34 is just below normal, with a carbon dioxide of 55 that is high and matches it. Bicarbonate is 30, a rise of about 5 (1.5 x 3.5), the size of chronic compensation. Obesity, sleep disordered breathing and chronic hypercapnia form the obesity hypoventilation triad. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with asthma notices that her wheezing and chest tightness flare at the same predictable point in every menstrual cycle and are mild the rest of the month. Which type of asthma describes this pattern?","choices":["Catamenial asthma","Aspirin-sensitive asthma","Occupational asthma","Intrinsic asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catamenial asthma is asthma whose symptoms occur at predictable times during the menstrual cycle, as with this woman's monthly flares. It is one of the other types of asthma alongside occupational, cardiac, exercise-induced and aspirin-sensitive patterns. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with asthma and a body mass index of 36 asks how his weight relates to his asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Obesity matters just for exercise-induced symptoms","Obesity has no link to asthma","Obesity makes asthma milder","Obesity makes asthma harder to control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is more frequent in people with a body mass index above 30, and their symptoms are more difficult to control, although the mechanism is unknown. His body mass index of 36 places him in that group. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old unvaccinated man with diabetes and heart failure has mild coronavirus disease 2019 that began 6 days ago. His oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Molnupiravir","A 3-day remdesivir course","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk outpatient with mild or moderate disease and no hypoxemia is offered nirmatrelvir/ritonavir within 5 days or a three-day intravenous remdesivir course within 7 days of symptom onset. At day 6 only the remdesivir window is still open, and his age, comorbidities and lack of vaccination make him high risk. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 113, 120, 122"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease, pulmonary hypertension and a hematocrit of 52% has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which finding makes her qualify?","choices":["Her hematocrit of 52%","Her interstitial lung disease","Nothing; 89% does not qualify","Her pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is just above the Group I cutoff of 88%, so she needs a comorbidity. Pulmonary hypertension is one of the listed qualifying findings, so it makes her qualify as Group II. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a single sputum culture that grows Mycobacterium avium complex at a routine visit. His symptoms are at baseline and no chest imaging has been done. Which best describes his status?","choices":["Nontuberculous lung disease","Latent tuberculosis infection","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Diagnostic criteria not met"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory isolate alone does not prove nontuberculous disease. Diagnosis requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging, exclusion of alternatives and usually 2 or more sputum cultures with the same species. He has one culture, no new symptoms and no imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 39"}]};
